#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Verify and fix metadata for a music library laid out as
<root>/<artist>/<album>/<song>.<ext>.

Artist/album are trusted from the folder structure. Title and track
number come from a MusicBrainz text search on that artist/album,
matched to the filename by track number or (failing that) fuzzy title.
AcoustID audio fingerprinting is a fallback for files that search can't
place -- fingerprints alone can't tell apart similar-sounding versions
of a song (e.g. instrumental vs. vocal) as reliably as the match above.

The recording's full artist credit (main + featured artists) is written
to a separate "artists" tag; the folder-trusted "artist" tag is untouched.

Recognition is slow and rate-limited, so nothing is written directly --
changes are queued to a YAML file for review, optionally sanity-checked
with --check, then applied with --apply. Neither calls the APIs.

--album-art is a separate mode: for each album, finds its MusicBrainz
release, fetches the cover from the Cover Art Archive, scales it to
300x300, and saves it as "art.jpg" (or a given filename), overwriting
any existing file.

A ".mmfignore" file in root (".gitignore" syntax) excludes matching
files/folders from every pass.

Setup:
    pip install mutagen pyacoustid musicbrainzngs pyyaml pillow pathspec
    # provides `fpcalc`, used by pyacoustid:
    sudo apt install libchromaprint-tools

    # Get a free AcoustID API key (no cost, just a signup):
    # https://acoustid.org/new-application
    export MMF_ACOUSTID_API_KEY=your_key_here

Usage:
    music_metafix /path/to/music/root
    music_metafix --check temp/changes.yml
    music_metafix /path/to/music/root --apply temp/changes.yml
    music_metafix /path/to/music/root --rename "%num - %title"
    music_metafix /path/to/music/root --album-art [filename]

Queue file paths are relative to root, so --apply needs the same root.
"""

